# {{name}}

{{description}}

A single page application (SPA) built with [Vite](https://vite.dev), [React](https://react.dev),
[TanStack Router](https://tanstack.com/router) and [TanStack Query](https://tanstack.com/query),
[Zustand](https://zustand.docs.pmnd.rs) and [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com). The build is a folder with
`index.html` plus JavaScript and CSS that any backend can serve.

## Commands

```bash
npm install
npm run dev         # dev server with hot reload on http://localhost:5173
npm run build       # type check and build into dist/
npm run preview     # serve the build locally
npm test            # vitest, `npm run test:watch` while working
npm run check       # format, lint (biome) and type check
```

## Structure

```
src/
  main.tsx             entry point: providers and router
  router.tsx           the router, uses the routes in src/routes
  routes/              one file per page (file based routing)
    __root.tsx         layout of all pages, the 404 page
    index.tsx          /
    about.tsx          /about
    items/index.tsx    /items
    items/$itemId.tsx  /items/:itemId, a route with a parameter and a loader
  routeTree.gen.ts     generated from src/routes by the Vite plugin, do not edit
  components/
    ui/                small reusable building blocks (Button, Card)
    layout/            the frame of the pages: header, navigation, footer
  features/<feature>/  everything of one topic: api/, components/, stores/
  lib/api-client.ts    fetch wrapper for the backend API
  lib/query-client.ts  TanStack Query settings
  stores/              global client state (Zustand), e.g. the theme
  styles/app.css       Tailwind and the design tokens (@theme)
  assets/images/       images used by the code or the CSS
  assets/fonts/        self hosted fonts (.woff2)
  test/                test setup and render helpers
  config.ts            settings that are the same in every environment
```

Rules of thumb:

- A new page is a new file in `src/routes`. Files starting with `-` there are not routes.
- Data from the backend goes through TanStack Query (see `features/health/api`), not into a store. It takes care of
  loading, caching, errors and refetching.
- Zustand stores hold state that only exists in the browser: theme, open dialogs, form drafts.
- Code that only one feature uses stays in that feature. Move it to `components/` or `lib/` once a second one needs it.
- `@/` is an alias for `src/`.

## Assets

There are two places for static files. Pick by how the file is used:

- **`src/assets/`** for everything the code or the CSS uses: `images/` (logos, illustrations, SVG icons) and `fonts/`
  (self hosted `.woff2`). Import them, e.g. `import logo from '@/assets/images/logo.svg'`, or use `url(...)` in CSS.
  The build adds a hash to the file name, so browsers can cache them forever, inlines small files and fails if a file
  is missing.
- **`public/`** only for files that need a fixed URL or are never referenced by the code: `favicon.svg`, `robots.txt`,
  social media preview images, `manifest.webmanifest`. They are copied as they are, without hash and without checks,
  and are referenced with an absolute path like `/favicon.svg`.

A self hosted font is wired up in `src/styles/app.css` (commented out): add the file, uncomment the `@font-face` and
set `--font-sans` in `@theme`.

## Tests

Unit and component tests run with [Vitest](https://vitest.dev) in a simulated browser (jsdom), components are tested
with [Testing Library](https://testing-library.com/docs/react-testing-library/intro). Tests are `*.spec.ts(x)` files
next to the code. `npm run test:watch` reruns the affected tests on save.

| What                        | How                                                              | Example                                 |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Plain functions and stores  | Call them and check the result, reset stores in `beforeEach`     | `features/counter/stores/*.spec.ts`     |
| Component without providers | `render()` from Testing Library                                  | `components/ui/button.spec.tsx`         |
| Component that uses the API | `renderWithProviders()` from `src/test/render.tsx`, stub `fetch` | `features/health/components/*.spec.tsx` |
| A page with routing         | `renderRoute('/path')` from `src/test/render.tsx`                | `router.spec.tsx`                       |

- Find elements like a user does: `screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' })`, `findBy...` waits for async content.
- Interact with the returned `user` (`await user.click(...)`, `await user.type(...)`), it behaves like a real user.
- No backend in tests: replace `fetch` with `vi.stubGlobal('fetch', vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(Response.json(...)))`.
- Matchers like `toBeInTheDocument()` come from jest-dom, set up in `src/test/setup.ts`.

## Configuration

Copy `.env.example` to `.env.local` and change what you need. `.env.local` is not committed.

| Variable            | Default     | Purpose                                                        |
| ------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `DEV_PORT`          | `5173`      | Port of the dev server and of `npm run preview`                |
| `DEV_HOST`          | `localhost` | Interface of the dev server, `0.0.0.0` in a container          |
| `BASE_PATH`         | `/`         | Path the app is served under, e.g. `/app/`                     |
| `OUT_DIR`           | `dist`      | Output of `npm run build`, e.g. `../server/web/dist`           |
| `API_PROXY_TARGET`  |             | Forward `/api` of the dev server to this backend               |
| `HMR_CLIENT_PORT`   |             | Port the browser uses for hot reload, see below                |
| `DEV_ORIGIN`        |             | Absolute URL of the dev server for asset URLs, see below       |
| `VITE_API_BASE_URL` | `/api`      | Base URL of the API as the browser sees it (part of the build) |

## Run it behind a backend

There are two ways to develop against a backend. Pick one.

**1. The backend proxies to Vite (recommended).** Open the backend, e.g. `http://localhost:8080`. It answers `/api` and
forwards every other request, including the hot reload websocket, to the Vite dev server. The browser sees one origin,
so there is no CORS and cookies work like in production. In Go:

```go
vite, _ := url.Parse("http://localhost:5173")
mux.Handle("/api/", apiHandler)
mux.Handle("/", httputil.NewSingleHostReverseProxy(vite)) // also forwards websockets
```

With a `BASE_PATH` like `/app/` forward `/app/` to Vite without removing the prefix. If hot reload does not connect
because the proxy does not forward websockets, set `HMR_CLIENT_PORT=5173` so the browser connects to Vite directly.
`DEV_ORIGIN` is only needed when the backend renders its own HTML and includes the Vite scripts.

**2. Vite proxies to the backend.** Open `http://localhost:5173` and set `API_PROXY_TARGET=http://localhost:8080`, Vite
then forwards `/api` to the backend.

## Deploy

`npm run build` writes static files to `dist/` (or `OUT_DIR`). Serve them with any web server or embed them into the
backend. Because the router runs in the browser, every path that is not a file must answer with `index.html` (SPA
fallback), so that reloading `/items/1` works. In Go:

```go
//go:embed all:dist
var dist embed.FS

func spa() http.Handler {
	files, _ := fs.Sub(dist, "dist")
	fileServer := http.FileServerFS(files)
	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		if _, err := fs.Stat(files, strings.TrimPrefix(path.Clean(r.URL.Path), "/")); err != nil {
			r.URL.Path = "/" // unknown path: serve index.html, the router shows the page or its 404
		}
		fileServer.ServeHTTP(w, r)
	})
}
```

Files in `dist/assets/` have a hash in their name and can be cached forever, `index.html` should not be cached.
